    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "It can be challenging when a patient's behavior feels unhinged and highly erratic. Here are some additional advice for responding in these cases:\n\n• Re-establish boundaries: Determine the appropriate physical distance for your team when interacting with the patient and reestablish professional boundaries while remaining calm. You can say, \"Let's talk from here.\" or \"I'd like to help with one thing at a time.\"\n\n• Use de-escalation tools: Redirect their energy with positive distractions like music, or show them a picture of their family or a comforting image. You can also provide them with a weighted blanket to help ground them and shift their perceptive reality.\n\n• Offer choices: Give them a choice to make it seem like their actions are under their control, increasing their autonomy. For instance, \"Would you like to listen to music, or would you like to talk to a friend?\"\n\n• Fatigue management: Acknowledge that energy depletion can contribute to the behavior; try to bring in staff who can match strength and energy to patients after medical evaluations or investigations.\n\n• Consult a psychiatrist for possible medications: In short bouts of severe agitation, you might consider a psychiatrist to discuss medication that could help to calm and focus the patient.\n\nThese steps could help your",
